# sync_timedb queue orchestrator contract

**Activation:** `alwaysApply: false` — when triggers in `agent-discipline-core.mdc` match, **Read this file before editing**. Triggered rules are mandatory, not optional.

Production coordinator for `sync_timedb` is **`run_sync_timedb_queue_orchestrator`** (wired from `run_sync_timedb_supervisor_from_parsed`). The B supervisor loop / `ArchiveJanitor` tick coordinator are **retired** — do not dual-run orchestrators.

## Laws

| Law | Detail |
|-----|--------|
| One process / `archive_dir` | Exclusive flock on `{archive_dir}/.sync_timedb_orchestrator.fnctl.lock` |
| Job kinds | In-process `SyncTimedbJobStore`: ingest ZSET (hot/catchup ranges), append/discover/day_close LISTs, deadline leases with **OQ-1 EX = per-file timeout max (default 86400) and no coordinator heartbeat renew**, in-flight map, path-only lease identity + payload fingerprint; dead-owner steal must requeue inflight |
| Atomic claim | In-process store claim (ranged pop + lease + in-flight) / LIST claim; never pop then lease. Fill paths must not call `pop_ingest_job_ranged` / `pop_list_job` |
| Reaper / steal | Tick `reap_expired_inflight`; boot **and** rate-limited runtime `steal_dead_owner_leases` **plus** `reconcile_this_owner_orphan_leases` when `len(local) < pool` and ingest ZSET is deep (SCAN, never KEYS) — not only when local maps are empty; host+boot scoped owner tokens; this-owner orphan leases (no local Future) are requeued |
| Streaming submit | Discover → `zadd_ingest` as records arrive from the walker pipeline. Since 2026-10-29 that pipeline is `fd | sort | tr` (see "Discover hands over oldest first"), so the first enqueue waits for the walk listing (about 2 s for 732k files), never for the per-file classify; **stop catchup enqueue at ingest queue cap**; **do not break the find walk** or refuse **hot-band** ZADD because day-close handoff filled catchup past `sync_ingest_queue_max_size`. Store `zadd_ingest` refuses a new catchup identity at `discover_ingest_capacity_limit` (`sync_ingest_queue_max_size`) for **discover only**; day-close handoff (`_handoff_retryable_paths_to_ingest`) and dead-letter readmit pass `over_cap=True` (DE-33: a refusal there was a silent drop that held the day open forever while `ingest_handoff=` counted success); new hot identities stay admitted past that cap. The member cap is not the catchup stop |
| Calendar day | Discover resolves day from ISO prefix or listend digit-epoch basename (`fromtimestamp`, never `rec.mtime`); **do not** substitute today. Unresolved day → skip ingest enqueue. Same-inode-as-`current` is not closed |
| Reserved ingest slots | `hot_cap = max(1, (2*pool)//3)`, `catchup_cap = pool - hot_cap` (catchup floored to 1 when `pool >= 2`). Reserved hot fill runs while **`hot_used < hot_cap`** and the pool has free slots (not `len(inflight) < hot_cap`). While **`hot_queued > 0`**, catchup fill ceiling stays **`catchup_cap`** — do **not** expand catchup to full pool because this tick submitted zero hot (`hot_submitted` ignored for expand). Catchup may use unused pool slots **only when the hot range is empty**. Elevated hot retry still runs when free slots remain and hot is queued. |
| Store inflight capacity (**RC8**) | Job-store ingest inflight count is pool-capacity authority; local `ingest_inflight` maps are a cache. When `queued>0` and (`inflight < pool` or `len(local) > inflight`), reconcile/prune phantoms missing from store inflight+lease (never prune live holders). Hygiene must **not** skip solely because `len(local) >= pool` when the store is underfull. Band caps use maintained `hot_used`/`catchup_used` counters (no per-claim full-map walk). Claim returns fingerprint (RC8b); multi-claim batch (RC8c); steal uses per-identity lookup not a full dump (RC8d); 0-submit deep-queue ticks sleep `<<50ms` (RC8e); census is `ingest_queue_census` |
| Reband | Recompute band at claim time and `zadd_ingest` the same identity with the new score deprioritized via `penalize_ingest_score` (RC9: deprioritize reband ping-pong) |
| Ingest score order (2026-10-28) | A queued ingest score is derived data: band + calendar day + hash tie-break (`encode_ingest_score`). **Every** deprioritizing requeue (fill backpressure skip, reband, expired lease) uses `penalize_ingest_score`, which clamps inside the file's own `SCORE_STRIDE` day slot, so no number of requeues can reorder days (oldest-day-first). Never add a whole-day penalty (the old `LEASE_CONFLICT_SCORE_PENALTY = SCORE_STRIDE` summed into 100–270-day offsets on hpcperfstats04, persisted in the snapshot, flattened ~110k queued files to three score-days and held every day-close at the live-ingest gate). Boot runs `_normalize_queued_ingest_scores` right after `rebuild_day_remainders_from_job_store` and before the first fill: a score outside its canonical day slot is re-derived (boot discover cannot repair it past `sync_ingest_queue_max_size`). Regression: `test_sync_timedb_ingest_score_order.py` |
| Seal failure visibility (2026-10-28) | `_seal_one_daily_tar` logs zstd's stderr with `Seal failed for` and returns False; `seal_dirty_daily_archives` returns the tar paths that failed; `_run_day_close_job` logs `stage_exit seal result=fail reason=seal_failed` for them (hpcperfstats04: seven zstd exit-27 seal failures on 08-22/08-23 logged `stage=seal result=ok` and no cause). Regression: `test_sync_timedb_seal_failure_visibility.py` |
| Handoff open-tar check once per batch (2026-10-28) | `_handoff_retryable_paths_to_ingest` with an open tar and no injected append predicate runs `_ensure_open_tar_readable_for_ingest_lookup` once and reads `get_mutable_tar_authority_member_map` once per batch; a same-size member is append-complete (`_open_tar_member_complete`). A members-store coordination error (append inflight) defers the whole batch with one rate-limited `handoff deferred tar= err= paths=` line and the raw stays for the next day-close claim. Before this, every path re-probed a 27-46 GB tar and logged `handoff fail` (hpcperfstats04: 132k lines for 51k paths). Regression: `test_sync_timedb_handoff_batch_check.py` |
| Seal deferred while raw is not in the tar (2026-10-28) | `_run_day_close_job` does not call `seal_dirty_daily_archives` when raw remains and `_remaining_raw_all_in_open_tar` is false (`seal_deferred`); it logs `stage_exit seal result=ok reason=deferred_raw_not_in_tar` and continues. The H19 `skip_merge` placeholder `{tar: ["skip_merge_remaining_raw"]}` is not a file, so `only_when_no_remaining_raw` could never defer the seal on that path (hpcperfstats04: 08-22/08-23 sealed seconds after `handoff_to_ingest n=4147/20938`, zstd read a growing tar: `Incomplete read`, exit 27). The sentinel is set before `raw_delete_pre_seal`, so when `closed_raw_paths_on_disk` then lists nothing the raw is gone and the seal runs (an empty listing is not "raw not in tar"; a missing lister still defers). Before this, 09-18 on hpcperfstats04 (manifest `done`, 0 raw, tar 10541 vs zst 10526 members) deferred every claim and failed `post_seal_verify` forever. Regression: `test_seal_with_raw_only_when_all_remaining_raw_in_tar`, `test_sentinel_with_no_raw_left_does_not_defer_the_seal` |
| Dead archive scratch sweep (2026-10-29) | `_idle_reconstruct_pass` calls `sweep_dead_archive_scratch` (`sync_timedb_archive_scratch.py`) after the A18 integrity sweep. It removes only `<day>.tar.{merge,dedupe,repair,decomp,zst}.tmp` regular files unmodified for `sync_archive_scratch_max_idle_hours` (default 24; 0 disables) while no append/restore is in flight for the day and the day's tar and `.decomp` write locks are both free (age re-checked under the locks). Names with no day get no day lock and are dead only when the newest mtime of the path **or anything beneath it** is that old, so a live writer keeps them young: `hps_{union_extract,pax_convert,tar_repair}_<8 chars>` staging directories (removed with `shutil.rmtree`; the original tar survives until the final `os.replace`, so they hold derived copies only) and sidecar write temps (`.atomic.<8 chars><name>` from `sync_timedb_persistence._save_json_atomic`, `*.json.tmp` from the integrity census and the salvage manifest). The sweep covers the daily dir and the archive dir plus their immediate `.sync_timedb_*` subdirectories, never `.sync_timedb_unparsable_raw` or `.sync_timedb_migration_backup`. `.corrupt.*`, `.pre-restore.*`, salvage backups, the real `.tar`/`.tar.zst`, sidecar data, lock sidecars and every other name never match; symlinks are not followed. `sync_dead_end_remediation_mode = report` logs `archive scratch would remove` and deletes nothing; each removal logs `archive scratch removed path= bytes= idle_h=`. Production signature: 94 GB of `merge.tmp` from Aug/Sep on hpcperfstats04. Regression: `test_sync_timedb_archive_scratch.py` |
| Full-archive rediscover (2026-10-29) | Discover skips (and forgets) every incomplete **catchup** file it sees while the ingest queue is at `sync_ingest_queue_max_size`, and the idle pass only walks `sync_ingest_rescan_mtime_days` (default 1), so a whole-archive walk used to run only at boot (hpcperfstats04: 732k raw files on disk, the full walk at the next restart found ~25k never-ingested files and reopened 64 days). `StreamingDiscoverStats.dropped_at_capacity` counts the skipped files; `_run_background_discover` sets `_FULL_REDISCOVER_PENDING` when it is non-zero. `_idle_reconstruct_pass` (non-force, windowed) runs a **whole-archive** discover (`mtime_days=None`) when `_full_rediscover_due` says so: the flag is set **or** `sync_ingest_full_rescan_interval_hours` (default 6; 0 disables the periodic walk) elapsed since the last one, **and** the catchup queue is at or below half the cap (so the walk is not dropped again). Logs `queue_orchestrator full rediscover catchup_queued= reason=dropped_at_cap\|periodic`. INV-NEG: the flag clears when the walk is submitted, re-sets only if that walk drops again, and a walk that drops nothing ends the chain. Regression: `test_sync_timedb_rediscover_and_codon_parsed.py` |
| Seal waits for the boot whole-archive walk (2026-10-29) | The seal sees only raw the manifest and queues know about (no disk find, H19/H22/H24), so a day sealed before the boot whole-archive discover reaches its hosts gets never-ingested raw appended afterwards and a full reseal (hpcperfstats04: 09-22, 09-28, 09-29, 10-06 resealed minutes after the walk reached c102-004/c102-005). Boot sets `_BOOT_FULL_DISCOVER_PENDING` where it queues the whole-archive identity; the walk's `finally` (`_run_background_discover`, or the `run_once` force pass with `mtime_days=None`) clears it whether the walk succeeds or raises. While set, `_run_day_close_job` logs `stage_exit seal result=yielded reason=full_discover_pending` and returns `yielded` (yield backoff, no attempt bump); pre-seal verify and `raw_delete_pre_seal` still run. Periodic walks do not gate seals. Regression: `test_sync_timedb_seal_after_full_discover.py` |
| Seal checks for unarchived raw (2026-10-29) | With an open tar, `_run_day_close_job` calls `_closed_raw_not_archived` after the `full_discover_pending` gate and before the seal: the census fd walk (`iter_find_stats_stdout_chunks`, streamed; host dirs ending in `host_name_ext` only, as in the discover filter; lock sidecars, dot-prefixed sidecar dirs and active `current` segments excluded) lists the day's closed raw by filename day (`calendar_day_from_find_record`); it must **not** use `build_day_scoped_closed_raw_by_gz`, which serializes on `_DAY_SCOPED_CLOSED_RAW_COLLECT_LOCK` behind verify passes and builds the whole listing (hpcperfstats04 2026-10-29: 08-27 waited 14+ min at `stage_enter seal`), and any file that is a same-size member of neither the open tar (`get_mutable_tar_authority_member_map`) nor the sealed member map goes to `_handoff_retryable_paths_to_ingest`; the job logs `stage_exit seal result=yielded reason=unarchived_raw n=` and returns `yielded`. A failed scan, or an empty member read of a tar larger than `_EMPTY_TAR_MAX_BYTES`, fails closed (`reason=unarchived_raw_check_failed`). No raw on disk returns before reading the tar. zst-only days (no open tar) skip the check (`no_tar_handoff` owns them). Before this the seal saw only raw the manifest and queues knew about (hpcperfstats04 2026-10-29: 08-25 sealed and deleted 9464 raw files with 189 never-queued files still on disk, then restored, appended and resealed). Regression: `test_seal_yields_and_requeues_raw_that_is_in_no_archive`, `test_unarchived_raw_is_in_neither_the_open_tar_nor_the_sealed_copy`, `test_unarchived_check_does_not_wait_on_the_shared_day_collect_lock` |
| Reconcile merges, the seal stage seals (2026-10-30) | Day-close `reconcile_merge` calls `reconcile_open_tar_with_sealed_zst(..., defer_seal=True)`: a tar that is a superset of its zst (`tar_superset_of_zst`, the normal state of a day restored for appends) or a divergent tar after its union merge (`divergent_maps`) returns `sealed=False` without compressing, so the `seal` stage seals it once behind `full_discover_pending`, the unarchived-raw check and `seal_deferred` (raw not yet in the tar). DE-9 `zst_unreadable_reseal` and `missing_zst` still seal in reconcile. Non-day-close callers keep the default (`defer_seal=False`). Production signature: hpcperfstats02 2026-10-30, 09-11 (322 GB) resealed by reconcile while its appends soft-requeued `open_tar_members_unavailable` for the whole compress, then needed a second full seal. Regression: `test_sync_timedb_reconcile_defer_seal.py` |
| Sealed-only reclassify reads the zst (2026-10-30) | `_reclassify_retryable_skips_on_disk` (reached from `apply_batch_delete` at `raw_delete_pre_seal` / `raw_delete` and the handoff reclassify) classifies every retryable raw path against the `.tar.zst` member map (`classify_removable_raw_paths_for_daily_gz`, `allow_auto_seal=False`, DB gate kept) when the day has a sealed file and no non-empty `.tar`; it no longer calls `ensure_daily_tar_restored_for_append`. Raw that is not a sealed member stays a retryable skip and goes to ingest/append, whose restore is gated at append dispatch. Days with an open tar keep the open-tar classify. `_pre_seal_verify_body` is only called by `_run_pre_seal_verify` when the tar exists. Production signature: hpcperfstats02 2026-10-30, day close restored 09-04 and 09-06 (300-600 GB each) to reclassify a few raw files. Regression: `test_reclassify_sealed_only_day_uses_zst_without_restore`, `test_reclassify_sealed_only_day_keeps_raw_missing_from_zst_skipped` |
| Space pressure yields growth to day close (2026-10-30) | `sync_archive_space_pressure_free_bytes` (X; `K`/`M`/`G`/`T` suffixes; default 1T; 0 = off) drives `sync_timedb_space_pressure.py`: pressure starts below X free on the daily archive filesystem and ends at 2X. Under pressure `_fill_append_slots` computes `space.select_append_day` once per tick: `None` (return 0, claim nothing) while any day close is in a finishing stage (`FINISHING_STAGES`, published by `_stage_enter` → `note_day_close_stage` and cleared in `_drain_day_close_ready`, both keyed by the day_close job identity so a bare-day and a tar-path job for one day cannot clear each other), else one day at a time (oldest parked day whose next batch fits; kept while it has an in-flight batch or parked appends that fit; a held day that stops fitting gives up the slot without a hold; `pre_seal_verify` is not a finishing stage — it yields after one classify batch per claim; the seal counts only after its `full_discover_pending` / `unarchived_raw` checks pass, which publish `seal_check` first), then a hold until a day close gives space back (`space.note_space_freed()` after `raw_delete_pre_seal` / `raw_delete` deleted raw, a seal that removed the open tar, or `tar_drop`; entering a finishing stage does not end it) or `DAY_CLOSE_START_GRACE_S` passes; `""` while nothing is parked (keep claiming). `_append_batch_need` keeps raw bytes + the sealed-day restore above `sync_archive_min_free_bytes` + half the restore, net of other reservations (gate on only); `space.reserve(tar, tar_path=tar)` before submit (released if `apply_async` raises), `space.release(key)` in `_drain_append_ready`, `space.reserving(..., tar_path=tar)` around `finish`/`growth` restores; a reservation counts only bytes its tar + `.decomp.tmp` have not grown by yet. Raw batch bytes come from `AppendDayClaimLists.batch_bytes` (one `getsize` per parked claim, dropped on pop). `_try_submit_pending_append_days(allowed_day=)` defaults to `_SELECT_APPEND_DAY_HERE`; None means appends wait. An unparsable INI value warns once and uses `1T` (`space_pressure_bytes_from_ini`). `ensure_daily_tar_restored_for_append(space_gate=)` (required keyword; a refusal returns the falsy `sp.SPACE_DENIED`, which `ensure_open_daily_tar_readable` and its salvage branch return as `transient=True, transient_reason="env:ENOSPC"` so the ingest lookup defers instead of setting `open_tar_gnu_tvf_failed`): `growth` (denied under pressure; ingest prewarm passes it explicitly with no restore days today), `finish` (day-close verify, `validate_open_tar_for_raw_removal`, `ensure_open_daily_tar_readable`, salvage: hard reserve only), `off` (append worker, gated at dispatch; a refusal there would burn a batch failure). `zstd_compress_tar_to_file` runs without nice/ionice under pressure. Census ` space=ok|pressure free= …`. Parked claims are never requeued for space. Production signature: hpcperfstats02 2026-10-30, 97% full, appends restoring 300-600 GB sealed days while three seals ran at 4.5 MB/s behind `ionice -n6`. Regression: `test_sync_timedb_space_pressure.py` |
| Discover hands over oldest first (2026-10-29) | The walk streamed in host-directory order, so the ingest cap (`sync_ingest_queue_max_size`) filled with files from every day and every touched day went through day close, reopened when the next walk found more of its files, and closed again (hpcperfstats04: 50k queued files across 08-16..10-29, 56 days restoring/appending/resealing every batch). `_boot_stream_discover` passes `sort_oldest_first=True`: `iter_find_stats_stdout_chunks` runs fd with `STAT_SORT_PRINTF_FORMAT` (one tab-separated NUL record per file) piped through GNU `sort -z -s -t/ -k<name field>n` (`LC_ALL=C`, filename epoch, oldest first) and `tr '\t' '\0'`, so the parser still reads four NUL fields; a non-zero `sort`/`tr` exit fails closed. Whole-archive walks use `FD_THREADS_WHOLE_ARCHIVE` (16) walker threads, windowed walks `FD_THREADS` (4). In `stream_enqueue_ingest_from_find_records`, at the cap an incomplete backfill file not already queued pushes out the youngest queued backfill file younger than its day (`SyncTimedbJobStore.evict_youngest_catchup_ingest`, lazy max-heap; hot and in-flight work is never pushed out; the day remainder is released); when nothing younger is queued its day becomes `frontier_day`, and later backfill records on or after the frontier count as dropped without being classified. A pushed-out file the walk did not queue again lowers the frontier to its day. Hot files join past the cap, so discover pushes out younger files until the older one fits, and `enqueue_reconstruct_jobs_for_closed_path` reports `ingest` only when the file is queued or in flight; a backfill file the store still refused (and that is not dead-lettered) counts as dropped and lowers the frontier to its day (hpcperfstats04 2026-10-29: the boot snapshot plus 377 hot files held the queue over the cap, one eviction per file never made room, and the ~313 oldest files, 189 of them for 08-25, were counted as queued and silently dropped; regression `test_sync_timedb_discover_refused_admission.py`). `_record_discover_frontier`: a whole-archive walk replaces `_discover_frontier_day`, a windowed walk only lowers it, a raising walk keeps it; logs `queue_orchestrator discover frontier day= previous= whole_archive=` on change. `_fill_day_close_slots` requeues without an attempt bump any backfill (non-hot) day on or after the frontier, and every backfill day while `_BOOT_FULL_DISCOVER_PENDING` is set (`_day_close_held_for_discover`), and stops the pass at the first repeated identity. INV-NEG: cleared by a whole walk that queues everything; retried by `_FULL_REDISCOVER_PENDING` walks at half the cap and the 6 h periodic walk; terminal when a walk drops nothing. Regression: `test_sync_timedb_discover_oldest_first.py` |
| Discover classify cost (2026-10-29) | The oldest-first walk classified ~7 files/s: py-spy of the discover thread showed 2 of 3 samples in `get_mutable_tar_authority_member_map` re-listing the 21 GB 08-25 open tar (appends kept changing it) and 1 in `stats_file_head_ingested_in_db`. `classify_closed_raw_path` looks up append completeness only once ingest is complete (append follows ingest; every consumer reads `needs_append` only when `needs_ingest` is false). `_boot_stream_discover` passes `ingest_is_complete_fn=jr.discover_ingest_is_complete` (file-complete / zero-host marks only, no head/tail DB probe); an unmarked file is queued and the ingest worker's head/tail check makes it `db_skip`. `maybe_record_file_complete_ingest_mark_from_outcome` marks a `head_tail`/`tail_window` `db_skip` while `listend_db_ingest_enabled` is off (the DB-before-archive case 3 proof; unreadable setting = on, no mark), so the next walk skips it instead of requeueing it until day close deletes the raw. Regression: `test_sync_timedb_discover_classify_cost.py` |
| Open-tar member scan failure is not an empty tar (2026-10-29) | `get_mutable_tar_authority_member_map` returns `{}` without caching it when the read-locked `tvf` fails with a non-`RuntimeError` (read-lock timeout under a seal); it used to cache `{}` under the tar's size+mtime, so every later append treated all its files as new until the tar changed. `_archive_stats_files_body` treats a `tar_scan` with no members on a tar larger than one 10240-byte record as a failed read: `INFO: archive_job soft_skip day= reason=open_tar_members_unavailable`, `ArchiveAppendOutcome(soft_requeue=True)`, no append attempt (hpcperfstats04: `to_add=2`, 60 s write-lock wait, `append fail err=TimeoutError` mid-seal; the same job after the seal had `to_add=0`). `_drain_append_ready` requeues every soft requeue through `_requeue_bounded_without_attempt_bump` (`env_defer`, `sync_noprogress_escalate_after`), not a bare `requeue_job`. Regression: `test_sync_timedb_append_members_unavailable.py` |
| Ingest tick order | ingest-coordinator: **fill → drain → fill** (same tick) so completions refill before sleep; then reap / idle poll |
| Populate pool | Orchestrator starts `PopulatePoolController` **before** boot discover submit, `reap_and_restart` each tick, `stop` on exit |
| Boot discover | After steal/reap and **after** ingest-pool + `populate.start()`, enqueue the configured incremental-rescan identity **before** the whole-archive identity, then `_submit_background_discover(..., mtime_days=rescan_mtime_days)` (same max-workers=1 executor as idle reconstruct). The worker must decode the scan window from the **claimed identity** (caller `mtime_days` is only a legacy/malformed-identity fallback), so a later incremental submit that claims the queued full identity actually runs the full catch-up. Current files are visible first without losing whole-archive catch-up. Log `queue_orchestrator boot discover submitted` **only after** `_submit_background_discover` returns. MainThread must **not** call `_boot_stream_discover` synchronously before the fill loop — that inlines sealed populate when the controller is still None (hpcperfstats03 2026-08-25: 13h `[main]` sealed `populate_source=*`, zero ingest-pool) |
| Discover bg Lock | `_submit_background_discover` creates the `ThreadPoolExecutor` and `submit`s **under one** `_discover_bg_lock` hold. **Never** call `_discover_executor()` while holding that lock — `_discover_bg_lock` is a non-reentrant `threading.Lock()`; nesting deadlocks MainThread forever (hpcperfstats03 2026-08-26: py-spy idle at `_discover_executor` under submit; discover LIST LLEN=1; fill never starts) |
| Discover subprocess stderr | A streaming stdout walker must not leave child stderr on an unread `PIPE`. Capture stderr to a non-pipe file (or drain concurrently) so the child cannot block; preserve stderr text for the fail-closed rc=1 `*.fnctl.lock` race distinction. On early generator close, close/kill before `wait()` so a full stdout PIPE cannot deadlock `discover-bg` (`stat` in `pipe_write` + parent `Popen.wait`) |
| MainThread allowlist | MainThread does **pool/thread maintenance only**: flock, boot steal/reap once, create pools, `populate.start()` / `reap_and_restart()`, start subsystem threads, boot discover submit, **pause-protocol** ingest pool recycle via `AtomicPoolRef`, fail-closed on unexpected coordinator death, shutdown `draining`→`drained` barrier. **Forbidden on MainThread:** `_fill_*`, `_drain_*`, `_idle_reconstruct_pass`, classify/find, `apply_async` |
| Subsystem ownership | One titled thread per kind: `ingest-coordinator` (ingest fill/drain/abandon + kind-scoped ingest reap + recycle request), `append-coordinator` (append fill/drain + **tar-deduped cheap day_close**; drain must honor `ArchiveAppendOutcome` gate-skip handoff + soft_requeue requeue), `day-close-coordinator` (day_close fill/drain into workers), `discover-bg` (unchanged Lock law), `reconstruct-coordinator` (idle reconstruct + census + **kind-scoped discover reap**; **H8/H21:** must not **submit discover** while the process-local discover Future is unfinished; store inflight can be transiently zero and is not a concurrency authority. It **must** still cheap-enqueue age-eligible open tars via `_enqueue_day_closes_for_daily_dir`; idle reconstruct throttle is **`_IDLE_RECONSTRUCT_MIN_INTERVAL_S = 300`**; H8/H21 busy skip does not burn the throttle). Fix every flaw **only** in the owning subsystem |
